MAME taptun (Linux): attach to a NAMED tap interface, not one global name.

WHY

Upstream hardcodes the tap device name as `tap-mess-<uid>-0`, so every MAME
process running as the same user competes for one interface — and MAME runs as
root here. The gallery needs a tap per tile (and namespaced taps for the clone
experiments under /data/vms/sandbox, which run beside the live exhibit), and
TUNSETIFF only ever attaches to the one name it is given.

`MAME_TAP_IFNAME` selects the interface. Unset keeps upstream's name byte for
byte, so this is inert for every other MAME use — the same env-gating rule
`IRIX_SHM_PATH` follows in mame-newport-shm-framebuffer.patch.

`m_ifname` is widened to IFNAMSIZ while we are here: at char[10] the existing
`strncpy` could neither hold a full-length interface name nor NUL-terminate one.

Note what this patch is NOT: the host interface a device is bound to is still
selected by MAME's machine cfg file (`<system><network><device tag interface/>`
in <cfg_directory>/<system>.cfg), which is what makes networking work at all
with `-video none` and no UI. This patch only decides WHICH host tap the
taptun provider's single device opens.

diff --git a/src/osd/modules/netdev/taptun.cpp b/src/osd/modules/netdev/taptun.cpp
index b765a667..ffc84f65 100644
--- a/src/osd/modules/netdev/taptun.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/modules/netdev/taptun.cpp
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include "util/hashing.h" // crc32_creator
 #include "util/unicode.h"
 
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <memory>
 #include <vector>
 
@@ -94,7 +95,7 @@ private:
 	bool m_receive_pending;
 #else
 	int m_fd = -1;
-	char m_ifname[10];
+	char m_ifname[IFNAMSIZ];
 #endif
 	uint8_t m_buf[2048];
 };
@@ -112,7 +113,14 @@ netdev_tap::netdev_tap(const char *name, network_handler &handler)
 	struct ifreq ifr;
 	memset(&ifr, 0, sizeof(ifr));
 	ifr.ifr_flags = IFF_TAP | IFF_NO_PI;
-	sprintf(ifr.ifr_name, "tap-mess-%d-0", getuid());
+	// Interface name: MAME_TAP_IFNAME when set, so several MAME instances on one
+	// host can each own their own tap device (upstream hardcodes ONE global
+	// name per uid). Unset keeps upstream behaviour byte for byte.
+	char const *const env_ifname = std::getenv("MAME_TAP_IFNAME");
+	if (env_ifname && *env_ifname)
+		snprintf(ifr.ifr_name, IFNAMSIZ, "%s", env_ifname);
+	else
+		snprintf(ifr.ifr_name, IFNAMSIZ, "tap-mess-%d-0", getuid());
 	if(ioctl(m_fd, TUNSETIFF, (void *)&ifr) == -1) {
 		osd_printf_verbose("tap: ioctl failed %d\n", errno);
 		close(m_fd);
@@ -120,7 +128,7 @@ netdev_tap::netdev_tap(const char *name, network_handler &handler)
 		return;
 	}
 	osd_printf_verbose("netdev_tap: network up!\n");
-	strncpy(m_ifname, ifr.ifr_name, 10);
+	snprintf(m_ifname, IFNAMSIZ, "%s", ifr.ifr_name);
 	fcntl(m_fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK);
 #elif defined(_WIN32)
 	std::wstring device_path(L"" USERMODEDEVICEDIR);
